159
00:08:51,850 --> 00:08:58,432
QA compares like with like, and the
designers never had to learn a single

160
00:08:58,455 --> 00:09:00,238
market rule 'cause that's not…

161
00:09:00,572 --> 00:09:02,063
They, they don't need to know this.

162
00:09:02,553 --> 00:09:06,120
People don't need to know every rule
because it's outside their purview.

163
00:09:06,331 --> 00:09:08,803
Their agents need to know where to ask

164
00:09:09,803 --> 00:09:13,880
The second customer can be a policy
agent because that's how we could

165
00:09:13,889 --> 00:09:15,765
define the guardrails really, right?

166
00:09:16,369 --> 00:09:20,225
So step one, a policy changes in
a company's compliance system of

167
00:09:20,236 --> 00:09:24,707
records, and then an agent asks the
platform for what's live in production.

168
00:09:25,417 --> 00:09:31,130
Next, it compares the two and finds where
production no longer matches the policy.

169
00:09:32,093 --> 00:09:35,919
It tells the owning team and opens
a new pull request with the fix.

170
00:09:36,705 --> 00:09:41,440
And step five, and the final step,
is the person reviews it and approves

171
00:09:41,441 --> 00:09:42,862
it before anything ships out.

172
00:09:42,873 --> 00:09:45,922
So now the end-to-end
process is done by an agent.

173
00:09:46,438 --> 00:09:51,116
A human is checking and actually
comparing it to the policy that is in

174
00:09:51,116 --> 00:09:53,341
question and then pushing it to live.

175
00:09:53,674 --> 00:09:58,054
Before, a policy change waited for someone
to notice for an audit or to find it.

176
00:09:58,078 --> 00:10:02,733
Now every policy change triggers
a check, and the fix shows up as a

177
00:10:02,733 --> 00:10:04,544
pull request, and you can review it

178
00:10:05,544 --> 00:10:07,501
Now I can hear the question.

179
00:10:07,604 --> 00:10:11,425
Agents opening pull requests on
compliance rule, who's in charge?

180
00:10:12,532 --> 00:10:14,111
This slide is an answer to that.

181
00:10:14,406 --> 00:10:16,172
Two loops are the road guardrails.

182
00:10:16,511 --> 00:10:19,700
Agents find and flag, people decide.

183
00:10:20,578 --> 00:10:22,175
Loop one is for teams.

184
00:10:22,729 --> 00:10:26,180
A teams agent sends its product
requirements to the platform.

185
00:10:26,860 --> 00:10:31,286
The platform sends back every
feature that needs market setup and

186
00:10:31,286 --> 00:10:33,451
every compliance rule that applies.

187
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:36,137
So the team builds them from day one.

188
00:10:36,145 --> 00:10:41,699
This is very, very similar to PRD, is
ingested, and it's ready to be coded.

189
00:10:42,852 --> 00:10:47,555
So you're automating the whole design,
process, design and architect process.

190
00:10:48,268 --> 00:10:50,741
The second loop is for
people who own the rules.

191
00:10:51,002 --> 00:10:54,355
Now, every month, anytime
something urgent comes up, an auto

192
00:10:54,355 --> 00:10:56,539
research loop scans the source.

193
00:10:56,757 --> 00:10:58,343
The rules depend on…

194
00:10:58,472 --> 00:11:02,647
Like it can be a standard body, a
government regulatory source, and it

195
00:11:02,648 --> 00:11:05,891
scores how well the rules still match.

196
00:11:05,924 --> 00:11:10,270
If the score drops below a threshold,
it flags it and proposes an update,

197
00:11:10,989 --> 00:11:15,522
and then a platform's custodian reviews
and approves every single change.

198
00:11:16,118 --> 00:11:17,903
Both loops meet in one place.

199
00:11:18,288 --> 00:11:22,951
The platform's rule set, teams
read it, custodians update it.

200
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:26,302
No rule change without
a person approving it

201
00:11:27,302 --> 00:11:30,858
And because people approve
every change, you get something

202
00:11:30,861 --> 00:11:33,584
compliance teams love, a trail.

203
00:11:34,811 --> 00:11:39,380
A lot of times we lose track of
when was this update made and why.

204
00:11:39,916 --> 00:11:42,643
The scan records which
source changed and when.

205
00:11:42,923 --> 00:11:45,668
Of course, a custodian approved
the update with a reason.

206
00:11:46,099 --> 00:11:51,358
The rule set gets a new version with
an effective date, and every answer

207
00:11:51,937 --> 00:11:56,857
a team gets it scoped to its market
and the name, the version behind it.

208
00:11:58,773 --> 00:12:02,738
So when an auditor asks, "Why did the
platform give the answer on what date?"

209
00:12:03,039 --> 00:12:05,611
You can show them the rule
version, the source behind it,

210
00:12:05,668 --> 00:12:06,905
and the person who approved it.

211
00:12:07,728 --> 00:12:10,417
The human approval is what
makes the trail credible.

212
00:12:11,936 --> 00:12:13,671
So what does this buy you?

213
00:12:14,549 --> 00:12:18,989
The impact shows up in three places,
one for each part of the fix.

214
00:12:19,311 --> 00:12:20,653
First, finding the rules.

215
00:12:21,075 --> 00:12:24,839
Requirements surface when the product
requirements are written, so compliance

216
00:12:25,080 --> 00:12:27,048
stop arriving at an escalation.

217
00:12:27,710 --> 00:12:29,557
Second, upgrades.

218
00:12:29,635 --> 00:12:32,758
Moving off an old version
becomes the platform's job.

219
00:12:33,234 --> 00:12:36,313
What took days now just
take, like, few hours.

220
00:12:36,313 --> 00:12:42,325
I've seen up to ninety-six percent of
reduction in time, but it depends, right?

221
00:12:42,881 --> 00:12:45,573
And teams also stop pushing
it back as tech debt.

222
00:12:46,448 --> 00:12:48,456
And third is keep the rules current.

223
00:12:49,367 --> 00:12:54,766
The monthly scan catches the source
chart, source change, and, a custodian

224
00:12:54,810 --> 00:12:59,478
actually approves it long before an
audit is being, this is being audited.

225
00:12:59,968 --> 00:13:04,914
So across all three, my directional
range is more than fifty…

226
00:13:05,540 --> 00:13:09,726
like somewhere between fifty to eighty
percent less effort and far fewer late

227
00:13:09,726 --> 00:13:12,252
surprises, and that's the bigger impact.

228
00:13:13,213 --> 00:13:16,814
Your number will differ, so measure
the things on the slide in your own

229
00:13:16,852 --> 00:13:19,334
platform and see where you land.

230
00:13:20,334 --> 00:13:25,804
If you want to try this, here are
five moves any platform team can copy.

231
00:13:26,441 --> 00:13:28,679
The first one, find your late loop.

232
00:13:29,050 --> 00:13:34,007
Ask yourself: Where do teams meet my
platform rules after the code is written?

233
00:13:34,584 --> 00:13:38,480
The second one, expose the
platform where work starts.

234
00:13:38,754 --> 00:13:43,354
Can an agent reach your platform from
the tools and your teams already use?

235
00:13:44,146 --> 00:13:48,042
Third, offer, adopt,
update, and verify tools.

236
00:13:48,278 --> 00:13:52,604
Can an agent build your platform,
hear what changed, and check its work?

